static const char _zs223_d[] = "reserved member name\n\nA type body declares a member named `public` or `tag`, or a unit declares a definition named `public`. `public` names the visibility block, so `public: unit { ... }` is the only declaration it may introduce; `tag` is the arm tag every sum type carries. Rename it.";
static const z_t83_t _zs223 = { .data = _zs223_d, .size = 285 };
static const char _zs224_d[] = "malformed require block\n\nA unit's `require:` block declares what the unit needs from the host toolchain. It must be a unit definition, must declare at least one requirement, and every entry must be a key the compiler consumes -- today only `library:`, whose value is a compile-time string naming a link library. An unknown or misspelt key would be silently ignored, so it is rejected.";
static const z_t83_t _zs224 = { .data = _zs224_d, .size = 384 };
static const char _zs225_d[] = "no main\n\nThe unit being compiled declares no `main: function is { ... }`, so the compiler has no entry point to start from. Compilation is demand-driven: `main` is the root, and what it reaches is what gets checked and emitted. A unit with no `main` is a library -- compile the program that opens it instead, or add a `main`. `--eager` resolves every definition rather than what a root reaches, so it does not require one.";
static const z_t83_t _zs225 = { .data = _zs225_d, .size = 422 };
